 \section{Contributions}
 \label{sec:intro:contributions}
 
 The contributions of this dissertation fall into four groups.
 
 \begin{description}
-  \item[Theoretical.] The finding that the input representation, together with the
-        sharing topology built on it, dominates whether multi-task learning helps
-        these POI prediction tasks. The arc also yields a corrected view of MTL for
-        this task family: a null result under a place-level representation coexists
-        with a positive result under a check-in-level one.
-  \item[Software.] The MTLnet framework (Chapter~\ref{ch:cbic}), the check-in-level
-        representation and the joint model built on it (Chapter~\ref{ch:mobiwac}), and the
-        reproducible training and evaluation pipelines behind the experiments of
-        Chapters~\ref{ch:cbic} and~\ref{ch:mobiwac}, with code repositories referenced in the respective
-        chapters.
-  \item[Empirical.] A six-dataset benchmark of joint versus dedicated models for next
-        category and next region prediction under user-disjoint cross-validation,
-        with twenty fitted models per configuration (four seeds, each a complete
-        repetition of the five-fold experiment with a different random initialization, over one
-        fixed set of five folds), paired significance tests on the four per-seed means, that is,
-        four paired observations, non-inferiority margins, and a leakage audit. All
-        four seeds reuse the same fold partition, so the reported intervals do not
-        cover uncertainty over resampled user splits.
